How the web client is delivered: assembled HTML, asset caching, the installed
app, and liveness across cache restores.

- Content-stamped assets: code-server keys static URLs by its repo commit,
  which this fork pins forever - the build takes COMPOSERY_STATIC_STAMP (the
  content hash build.sh computes) instead. The served HTML is
  Cache-Control: no-store because it embeds stamped URLs; service workers
  register with updateViaCache 'none'; each boot purges service workers and
  vscode-resource-cache entries stranded under old stamps - storage a mobile
  WebView user has no way to clear.
- Overlay asset wiring: the workbench pages link narrow.css / touch.css /
  fonts.css and load narrow.js / touch.js from workbench-assets, carry the
  shared viewport contract (viewport-fit=cover,
  interactive-widget=resizes-content), and declare the favicon set: sized ICO
  fallback first, adaptive SVG last, with favicon.js swapping the
  scheme-pinned pair (Chromium rasterizes a favicon once per URL and never
  re-runs its embedded media query).
- The installed app: standalone display, not fullscreen - the clock and
  battery stay visible, and window-controls-overlay is dropped with it because
  our titlebar reserves no titlebar-area-* room. Brand name (the
  apple-mobile-web-app-title iOS actually shows), description and colours are
  pinned to packages/shared by the "PWA install metadata" test. The manifest
  carries one colour for the Android splash (the dark tile of the launcher
  icon); theme-color metas then track the scheme per prefers-color-scheme.
- bfcache: a back/forward-cache restore hands back the DOM with every socket
  dead, so the workbench looks alive over a dead connection. no-store used to
  keep pages out of the bfcache; Chrome on Android now caches them anyway, so
  the page reloads itself on a persisted pageshow - `persisted` is set on a
  bfcache restore and nothing else.

- Webview pre pages (the iframes extensions render into): the same viewport
  contract (viewport-fit=cover, interactive-widget=resizes-content) plus
  safe-area padding, kept in place at runtime for documents webviews replace
  (ensureMobileViewport), with the CSP hash updated for the edited inline
  script.

